# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Unofficial machine-generated working translation of Dominus ac Redemptor
# (Clement XIV, 21 July 1773), made for this site directly from the Latin of
# Theiner's edition (1852), paragraph by paragraph on Theiner's numbering.
# No existing translation was consulted. Dedicated to the public domain
# under CC0 1.0 — cite the Latin.

EN = {
1: "Our Lord and Redeemer Jesus Christ, foretold by the Prophet as the Prince of Peace — which, coming into this world, he made known first to the shepherds through angels, and at the last, before he ascended into heaven, left once and again himself to his disciples — when he had reconciled all things to God the Father, making peace through the blood of his cross for whatever is on earth and whatever is in heaven, entrusted to the Apostles also the ministry of reconciliation and placed in them the word of reconciliation, so that, serving as ambassadors for Christ — who is not a God of dissension but of peace and of love — they might announce peace to the whole world, and might above all direct their labours and endeavours to this: that all who are begotten in Christ should be careful to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace, one body and one spirit, even as they were called in the one hope of their calling — to which, as Saint Gregory the Great says, there is no attaining at all unless one strives toward it with a mind united to one's neighbours.",
2: "This very word and ministry of reconciliation, divinely committed to Us with the greater force, We called to mind as soon as We were raised — with merits entirely unequal to it — to this See of Peter; We have kept it before our eyes day and night, and bearing it inscribed deep in our heart We have striven to answer to it with all our strength, imploring the divine help unceasingly, that God might deign to pour thoughts and counsels of peace into Us and into the whole flock of the Lord, and to open to Us the safest and firmest way of attaining it. Knowing well, moreover, that We have been set by God's counsel over nations and over kingdoms so that, in tilling the vineyard of Sabaoth and preserving the edifice of the Christian religion whose cornerstone is Christ, We might root up and pull down, scatter and destroy, and build and plant, We have ever been of this mind and constant will: that as We have judged that nothing must be neglected by Us for the quiet and tranquillity of the Christian commonwealth which could in any way serve for planting and building, so — when the same bond of mutual charity demands it — We should be equally prompt and prepared to root up and destroy even what is most pleasing and most dear to Us, and what We could scarcely lose without the greatest distress and sorrow of mind.",
3: "It is surely beyond doubt that among the things which contribute most to procuring the good and the happiness of the Catholic commonwealth, almost the first place must be given to the regular orders, from which in every age the amplest ornament, protection and profit have flowed out upon the whole Church of Christ. For this reason this Apostolic See has not only approved them and supported them with its patronage, but has also enriched them with many benefits, exemptions, privileges and faculties, so that they might be more and more stirred and inflamed to cultivate piety and religion, to form the morals of the peoples rightly by word and example, and to preserve and confirm the unity of the faith among the faithful. But when things have come to such a pass that from some regular order those most abundant fruits and most desired advantages for which it was first instituted were no longer received by the Christian people, or that it seemed rather to be a detriment, and fitter to disturb the tranquillity of the peoples than to procure it, this same Apostolic See — which had spent its labour on planting them and had lent them its authority — has never hesitated either to fortify them with new laws, or to recall them to their original severity of life, or even to root them out and disperse them altogether.",
4: "For this very cause Innocent III, our predecessor, when he had found that the excessive diversity of the regular orders was bringing grave confusion into the Church of God, firmly forbade in the Fourth Lateran general council that anyone thereafter should devise a new religious institute; whoever wished to be converted to religion was to take up one of those approved; and he decreed further that whoever wished to found a religious house anew should receive a rule and institute from those approved. Whence it followed that it was in no way lawful to institute a new religious order without the special licence of the Roman Pontiff — and with good reason: for since new congregations are instituted for the sake of greater perfection, the form of their future life ought first to be examined and weighed diligently by this holy Apostolic See itself, lest under the appearance of a greater good and a holier life very many inconveniences, and perhaps even evils, arise in the Church of God.",
5: "But although these things had been most providently established by Innocent III our predecessor, nevertheless afterwards not only did the importunate craving of petitioners extort from the Apostolic See the approval of certain regular orders, but the presumptuous rashness of some devised an almost unbridled multitude of different orders, chiefly mendicant, not yet approved. When this was fully known, Gregory X, likewise our predecessor, that he might meet the evil at once, renewed in the general council of Lyons the constitution of Innocent III and more strictly forbade anyone thereafter to devise a new order or religious institute, or to assume the habit of a new institute. All religious institutes and mendicant orders generally, devised after the Fourth Lateran council, which had earned no confirmation from the Apostolic See, he prohibited in perpetuity; those confirmed by the Apostolic See he decreed should subsist only in the manner following: namely, that the professed of those orders might remain in them, if they wished, on condition that they admit no one further to their profession, acquire no new house or place, and have no power to alienate the houses or places they held without the special licence of the same Holy See. For he reserved all these things to the disposition of the Apostolic See, to be converted to the aid of the Holy Land, or to the poor, or to other pious uses, through the ordinaries of the places or those to whom the Holy See itself should commit it. To the persons of those orders he entirely forbade, as regards those outside, the office of preaching and of hearing confessions, and burial also. He declared, however, that the orders of Preachers and Minors were by no means comprehended in this constitution, since the evident benefit coming from them to the universal Church had shown them approved. He willed moreover that the orders of the Hermits of Saint Augustine and of the Carmelites remain in solid state, because their institution had preceded the said Lateran general council. Finally, to individual persons of the orders to which this constitution extended he granted a general licence of passing to the other approved orders — yet so that no order might transfer itself wholly to another, or convent to convent, with its places, without first obtaining the special licence of the Apostolic See.",
6: "In these same footsteps, according to the circumstances of the times, followed other Roman Pontiffs our predecessors, whose decrees it would be long to recount. Among the rest, Clement V, likewise our predecessor, by his letters under lead expedited the sixth of the nones of May in the year of the Lord's Incarnation 1312, suppressed and totally extinguished the military order called of the Templars — although it had been lawfully confirmed, and had otherwise deserved so excellently of the Christian commonwealth that it had been laden by the Apostolic See with signal benefits, privileges, faculties, exemptions and licences — on account of its universal defamation; even though the general council of Vienne, to which he had committed the examination of the affair, judged that it should abstain from pronouncing a formal and definitive sentence.",
7: "Saint Pius V, likewise our predecessor, whose eminent holiness the Catholic Church devoutly honours and venerates, extinguished and utterly abolished the regular order of the Humiliati Brothers — older than the Lateran council, and approved by Innocent III, Honorius III, Gregory IX and Nicholas V of happy memory, Roman Pontiffs likewise our predecessors — for disobedience to apostolic decrees, for domestic and external discords that had arisen, because it gave no promise whatever of future virtue, and because certain men of that order wickedly conspired to murder Saint Charles Borromeo, Cardinal of the Holy Roman Church, protector and apostolic visitor of the said order.",
8: "Urban VIII, of honoured memory, also our predecessor, by his letters in like form of Brief expedited the 6th of February 1626, perpetually suppressed and extinguished the Congregation of the Reformed Conventual Brothers, solemnly approved by Sixtus V of happy memory, likewise our predecessor, and enriched with many benefits and favours — because from those brothers spiritual fruits had not come forth in the Church of God, and indeed very many differences had arisen between the Reformed Conventual Brothers and the Conventual Brothers not Reformed. The houses, convents, places, furnishings, goods, effects, actions and rights belonging to the said congregation he granted and assigned to the Order of Friars Minor Conventual of Saint Francis, excepting only the Neapolitan house and the house of Rome called of Saint Anthony of Padua, which last he applied and incorporated to the Apostolic Camera, and reserved to his own disposition and that of his successors; and to the brothers of the said suppressed congregation he permitted passage to the Brothers of Saint Francis called Capuchins, or those of the Observance.",
9: "The same Urban VIII, by other letters of his in equal form of Brief expedited the 2nd of December 1643, perpetually suppressed, extinguished and abolished the regular Order of Saints Ambrose and Barnabas ad Nemus, subjected the regulars of the said suppressed order to the jurisdiction and correction of the ordinaries of the places, and granted the said regulars licence to transfer themselves to other regular orders approved by the Apostolic See. This suppression Innocent X of honoured memory, also our predecessor, solemnly confirmed by his letters under lead expedited the Kalends of April in the year of the Lord's Incarnation 1645; and moreover he reduced to the secular state the benefices, houses and monasteries of the said order, which before were regular, and declared that they should thereafter be secular.",
10: "And the same Innocent X, our predecessor, by his letters in like form of Brief of the 16th of March 1645, on account of the grave disturbances stirred up among the regulars of the Order of the Poor of the Mother of God of the Pious Schools — although that order, after previous mature examination, had been solemnly approved by Gregory XV our predecessor — reduced the said regular order to a simple congregation without the taking of any vows, after the pattern of the institute of the Congregation of secular priests of the Oratory in the church of Santa Maria in Vallicella in Rome, called of Saint Philip Neri; granted the regulars of the order so reduced passage to any approved religious institute; forbade the admission of novices and the profession of those admitted; and finally transferred wholly to the ordinaries of the places the superiority and jurisdiction which had resided with the minister general, the visitors and the other superiors. All of which took effect for some years, until at length this Apostolic See, having recognised the usefulness of the said institute, recalled it to the original form of solemn vows and restored it to a perfect regular order.",
11: "By his like letters in equal form of Brief expedited the 29th of October 1650, the same Innocent X our predecessor, likewise because of discords and dissensions that had arisen, totally suppressed the Order of Saint Basil of the Armenians; he subjected the regulars of the said suppressed order in every way to the jurisdiction and obedience of the ordinaries of the places, in the habit of secular clerics, a suitable maintenance being assigned to them from the revenues of the suppressed convents; and he also granted them the faculty of passing to any approved religious institute.",
12: "Likewise the same Innocent X our predecessor, by other letters of his in the said form of Brief expedited the 22nd of June 1651, considering that no spiritual fruits could be hoped for in the Church from the regular Congregation of the Priests of the Good Jesus, extinguished the said congregation in perpetuity; subjected the said regulars to the jurisdiction of the ordinaries of the places, a suitable maintenance being assigned them from the revenues of the suppressed congregation, and with the faculty of passing to any regular order approved by the Apostolic See; and reserved to his own judgement the application of the goods of the said congregation to other pious uses.",
13: "Finally Clement IX of happy memory, likewise our predecessor, when he had observed that three regular orders — namely the Canons Regular of Saint George in Alga, the Hieronymites of Fiesole, and lastly the Jesuati founded by Saint John Colombini — brought little or nothing of usefulness or advantage to the Christian people, nor could be hoped ever to bring it, took counsel to suppress and extinguish them, and carried it out by his letters in like form of Brief expedited the 6th of December 1668; and their goods and quite considerable revenues, at the petition of the Republic of Venice, he willed to be spent on those burdens which had of necessity to be undertaken to sustain the Cretan war against the Turks.",
14: "In deciding and carrying through all these things, our predecessors judged it always better to employ that most well-advised manner of proceeding which they considered more apt to close off altogether every opening for contentions of spirit, and to remove all disputes and partisanships whatsoever. Hence, setting aside that laborious and business-laden method which is customarily employed in the conduct of judicial proceedings, and adhering solely to the laws of prudence, they took care to settle the whole matter by that fullness of power with which, as Vicars of Christ on earth and supreme moderators of the Christian commonwealth, they are most amply endowed — without granting the regular orders destined for suppression leave and faculty to try their rights, or to repel those most grave accusations, or to remove the causes on account of which they were led to adopt that kind of counsel.",
15: "With these and other examples of the greatest weight and authority before our eyes, and burning at the same time with a vehement desire to walk with confident mind and sure foot in the deliberation We shall disclose below, We omitted no diligence and no inquiry in searching out whatever pertains to the origin, the progress and the present state of the regular order commonly called the Society of Jesus; and We found thereby that it was instituted by its holy founder for the salvation of souls, for the conversion of heretics and above all of the infidels, and finally for the greater increase of piety and religion, and that, the more easily and happily to attain that most desired end, it was consecrated to God by the strictest vow of evangelical poverty, in common as well as in particular — excepting only the colleges of studies or letters, to which the power and capacity of possessing revenues was so given that nothing of those revenues might ever be spent or converted to the advantage, use or benefit of the Society itself.",
16: "Under these and other most holy laws the same Society of Jesus was first approved by Paul III of honoured memory, our predecessor, by his letters under lead expedited the 5th of the Kalends of October in the year of the Lord's Incarnation 1540, and by the same was granted to it the faculty of framing laws and statutes by which the protection, safety and government of the Society should be most firmly provided for. And although the same Paul our predecessor had at the beginning confined the Society within the very narrow limit of no more than sixty members, yet by other letters of his likewise under lead, expedited the day before the Kalends of March in the year of the Lord's Incarnation 1543, he made room in the same Society for all whom its moderators should judge it opportune or necessary to receive into it. Then in the year 1549, by his letters in like form of Brief expedited the 15th of November, the same Paul our predecessor endowed the same Society with many and most ample privileges — and among them willed and commanded that the indult, granted before by him to the superiors general of the said Society, of admitting twenty priests as spiritual coadjutors and of imparting to them the same faculties, grace and authority as are given to the professed members themselves, be extended without any limit or number to any others whomsoever whom the same superiors general should judge fit; and moreover he exempted and set free the Society itself, and all its members and persons, and all their goods whatsoever, from all superiority, jurisdiction and correction of any ordinaries whomsoever, and took them under his own protection and that of the Apostolic See.",
17: "No less was the liberality and munificence of the rest of our predecessors toward the same Society. For it is well known that by Julius III, Paul IV, Pius IV and V, Gregory XIII, Sixtus V, Gregory XIV, Clement VIII, Paul V, Leo XI, Gregory XV, Urban VIII of honoured memory, and by other Roman Pontiffs, the privileges already bestowed on the same Society were either confirmed, or increased with new additions, or most plainly declared. Yet from the very tenor and words of the apostolic constitutions it is openly gathered that in the same Society, almost from its beginning, various seeds of dissensions and rivalries sprouted — not only among the members themselves, but also with other regular orders, with the secular clergy, with academies, universities and public schools of letters, and even with the princes in whose dominions the Society had been received; and that these contentions and disputes were stirred up now about the character and nature of the vows, about the time of admitting members to vows, about the faculty of expelling members, about promoting members to sacred orders without a title and without solemn vows contrary to the decrees of the Council of Trent and of Pius V our predecessor of holy memory; now about the absolute power which the superior general of the same Society claimed for himself, and about other matters touching the government of the Society; now about various heads of doctrine, about schools, about exemptions and privileges which the ordinaries of the places and other persons constituted in ecclesiastical or secular dignity contended were injurious to their jurisdiction and rights; nor, lastly, were there wanting most grave accusations brought against the same members, which disturbed not a little the peace and tranquillity of the Christian commonwealth.",
18: "Hence arose many complaints against the Society which, fortified by the authority of certain princes as well, were laid before Paul IV, Pius V and Sixtus V, our predecessors of honoured memory. Among these was Philip II, the Catholic King of the Spains, of illustrious memory, who took care to lay before the same Sixtus V our predecessor both the most weighty reasons by which he was urged, and the complaints which he had received from the inquisitors of the Spains against the immoderate privileges of the Society and its form of government, together with the heads of contention confirmed by some men of the same Society most distinguished for learning and piety; and he pressed upon him that he should decree and commission an apostolic visitation of the Society.",
19: "The same Sixtus our predecessor assented to the petitions and endeavours of King Philip, which he had observed to rest on the highest equity, and chose for the office of apostolic visitor a bishop most commended to all for prudence, virtue and learning, and moreover designated a congregation of several Cardinals of the Holy Roman Church who should devote their diligent labour to carrying the matter through. But the said Sixtus V our predecessor being snatched away by an untimely death, the most salutary design he had undertaken vanished and was without any effect. Raised then to the supreme summit of the apostolate, Gregory XIV of happy memory, by his letters under lead expedited the 4th of the Kalends of July in the year of the Lord's Incarnation 1591, again most amply approved the Institute of the Society and ordered that all privileges whatsoever conferred on the same Society by his predecessors be held ratified and firm — and, before the rest, that one by which it had been provided that members might be expelled and dismissed from the Society without any judicial form, that is, with no previous inquiry, no drawing up of acts, no judicial order observed, no terms even substantial observed, on inspection of the truth of the fact alone, account being taken only of the fault or of a reasonable cause, and of the persons and other circumstances. He imposed moreover the deepest silence, and forbade, above all under pain of excommunication latae sententiae, that anyone should dare to attack the Institute, constitutions or decrees of the said Society directly or indirectly, or should endeavour to have anything about them changed in any way; leaving nevertheless to everyone the right to signify and propose whatever he should judge ought to be added, diminished or changed, but only to himself and to the Roman Pontiffs for the time being, either immediately or through the legates or nuncios of the Apostolic See.",
20: "But so far were all these things from sufficing to quiet the outcries and complaints against the Society that rather the most troublesome contentions spread more and more over almost the whole world — concerning the doctrine of the Society, which very many denounced as repugnant to the orthodox faith and to good morals; dissensions domestic and external boiled up; and the accusations against it multiplied, above all of excessive greed for earthly goods. From all of which took their origin both those disturbances well enough known to all, which brought the Apostolic See great sorrow and trouble, and the measures which certain princes adopted against the Society. So it came about that the same Society, when it would obtain a new confirmation of its Institute and privileges from Paul V of happy memory our predecessor, was compelled to ask of him that he would ratify and confirm with his authority certain decrees published in the fifth general congregation and transcribed word for word into his letters expedited thereon under lead the day before the Nones of September in the year of the Lord's Incarnation 1606; in which decrees it is most plainly read that it was as much the internal quarrels and disturbances of the members as the complaints and demands of outsiders against the Society that drove the members gathered in assembly to enact the following statute: 'Since our Society, which the Lord has raised up for the propagation of the faith and the winning of souls, can happily attain by the proper ministries of its Institute, which are spiritual arms, under the standard of the cross, with benefit to the Church and edification of neighbours, the end at which it aims, so also it would both hinder these goods and expose itself to the greatest dangers if it should handle the things that are secular and that pertain to politics and the government of states; therefore it was most wisely established by our elders that, warring for God, we should not be entangled in other things abhorrent to our profession. And since especially in these most perilous times, in many places and with various princes — whose love and charity Father Ignatius of holy memory thought it belonged to the divine service to preserve — our religious institute may perhaps through the fault of some, and either through ambition or indiscreet zeal, be ill spoken of, while the good odour of Christ is otherwise necessary for bearing fruit, the congregation has judged that we must abstain from every appearance of evil, and must meet complaints as far as may be, even those proceeding from false suspicions. Wherefore by the present decree it gravely and severely forbids all of ours to involve themselves in public affairs of this kind by any means, even if invited or drawn in, or to swerve from the Institute for any entreaties or persuasions. And further it has commended to the father definitors to determine and define with care by what more efficacious remedies a medicine may be applied wholly to this disease, wherever there be need.'",
21: "With the greatest sorrow of our soul We have observed that both the aforesaid and many other remedies applied thereafter carried almost no force or authority to uproot and scatter so many and so great troubles, accusations and complaints against the oft-named Society, and that our other predecessors laboured in vain to this end — Urban VIII, Clement IX, X, XI and XII, Alexander VII and VIII, Innocent X, XI, XII and XIII, and Benedict XIV — who endeavoured to restore to the Church her longed-for tranquillity by publishing many most salutary constitutions: whether concerning secular business, not to be engaged in either outside the sacred missions or on their occasion; or concerning the most grave quarrels and disputes sharply stirred up by the Society against the ordinaries of the places, the regular orders, pious places and communities of every kind in Europe, Asia and America, not without immense ruin of souls and astonishment of the peoples; or again about the interpretation and practice of certain heathen rites employed here and there in various places, to the omission of those which the universal Church rightly approves; or about the use and interpretation of those maxims which the Apostolic See has deservedly proscribed as scandalous and manifestly injurious to good morals; or finally about other matters of the greatest moment and altogether necessary for preserving intact the purity of Christian doctrine, from which in our own age no less than in the former very many detriments and inconveniences flowed: disturbances, namely, and tumults in some Catholic countries, persecutions of the Church in certain provinces of Asia and Europe; and lastly great grief was brought to our predecessors, and among them to Innocent XI of pious memory, who, compelled by necessity, went so far as to forbid the Society to admit novices to the habit; then to Innocent XIII, who was forced to threaten it with the same penalty; and finally to Benedict XIV of honoured memory, who judged that a visitation must be decreed of the houses and colleges lying in the dominions of our most dear son in Christ the Most Faithful King of Portugal and the Algarves; nor did there thereafter accrue any comfort to the Apostolic See, any help to the Society, or any good to the Christian commonwealth from the latest apostolic letters — extorted rather than obtained, to use the word which our predecessor Gregory X employed in the above-cited ecumenical council of Lyons — from Clement XIII of happy memory, our immediate predecessor, in which the Institute of the Society of Jesus is greatly commended and approved anew.",
22: "After so many and so great storms and most bitter tempests, every good man hoped that that most longed-for day would at length dawn which should bring tranquillity and peace in fullest measure. But under the government of the same Clement XIII our predecessor upon the Chair of Peter, times far more difficult and more turbulent supervened. For as the outcries and complaints against the said Society daily increased, and indeed as most perilous seditions, tumults, discords and scandals broke out in some places, which — the bond of Christian charity being loosened and utterly torn apart — vehemently inflamed the minds of the faithful to partisanship, hatred and enmity, the danger and peril seemed brought to such a point that those very princes whose ancestral piety and liberality toward the Society, received as it were by hereditary right from their forebears, is praised by almost every tongue — namely our most dear sons in Christ the Kings of France, of the Spains, of Portugal and of the Two Sicilies — were absolutely compelled to dismiss and expel from their kingdoms, dominions and provinces the members of the Society, judging this one extreme remedy to remain against so many evils, and altogether necessary to prevent the Christian peoples from challenging, provoking and rending one another in the very bosom of holy Mother Church.",
23: "But the said our most dear sons in Christ, holding it certain that this remedy could not be firm, nor apt to reconcile the whole Christian world, unless the Society itself were utterly extinguished and suppressed entire, laid their desires and their will before the said Clement XIII our predecessor, and with what authority and entreaties they could, their petitions joined together, demanded that he would by this most efficacious means make provident and final provision for the perpetual security of their subjects and for the good of the universal Church of Christ. But the death of the same Pontiff, falling out contrary to the expectation of all, wholly impeded the course and issue of the affair. Hence, when We had been placed by the disposing divine Clemency upon the same Chair of Peter, the same prayers, petitions and desires were at once laid before Us; and to them very many bishops, and other men most conspicuous for dignity, learning and religion, added their own endeavours and the judgement of their minds.",
24: "That in a matter so grave and of such moment We might take the safest counsel, We judged that We needed a long space of time, not only that We might inquire diligently, weigh maturely and deliberate most advisedly, but also that with many groanings and continual prayers We might beg from the Father of lights his singular help and protection; in which matter We also took care to be aided often before God by the prayers and works of piety of all the faithful. We willed among other things to search out on what foundation rests that opinion spread among very many, that the religious institute of the Clerks of the Society of Jesus was in some solemn manner approved and confirmed by the Council of Trent; and We found that nothing else was done concerning it in the said council than that it was excepted from that general decree by which it was provided, for the other regular orders, that at the end of the time of novitiate the novices found fit should be admitted to profession or dismissed from the monastery. Wherefore the same holy synod (Session 25, chapter 16, On Regulars) declared that it did not wish to make any innovation or prohibition that would prevent the said religious institute of the Clerks of the Society of Jesus, according to their pious Institute approved by the holy Apostolic See, from being able to serve the Lord and his Church.",
25: "Having therefore employed so many and so necessary means, aided, as We trust, by the presence and inspiration of the Divine Spirit, and compelled also by the necessity of our office, by which We are most strictly bound, so far as our strength allows, both to procure, foster and strengthen the quiet and tranquillity of the Christian commonwealth, and to remove utterly from its midst whatever can be even the least detriment to it; and since We have moreover observed that the said Society of Jesus can no longer bring forth those most abundant and most ample fruits and benefits for which it was instituted, approved by so many of our predecessors, and adorned with so many privileges, and that indeed it can hardly or not at all come to pass that, the Society remaining unharmed, the true and lasting peace of the Church be restored: moved therefore by these most grave causes, and pressed by other reasons which the laws of prudence and the best government of the universal Church supply to Us, and which We keep stored deep in our mind, following in the footsteps of the same our predecessors, and especially of the said Gregory X our predecessor in the general council of Lyons — since the present matter also concerns a Society reckoned, both by reason of its Institute and by reason of its privileges, among the mendicant orders — with mature counsel, of our certain knowledge and out of the fullness of apostolic power, WE EXTINGUISH AND SUPPRESS THE OFT-NAMED SOCIETY: We take away and abrogate each and all of its offices, ministries and administrations, houses, schools, colleges, hospices, gymnasia and places whatsoever, in whatever province, kingdom or dominion existing and in any way belonging to it; its statutes, usages, customs, decrees and constitutions, even though strengthened by oath, apostolic confirmation or otherwise; likewise each and all of its privileges and indults, general or special, the tenors of which We will to be held as fully and sufficiently expressed in these presents as if they were inserted word for word, howsoever conceived in whatever formulas, clauses of annulment, bonds and decrees. And therefore We declare that all and every authority of the superior general, the provincials, the visitors and any other superiors whatsoever of the said Society, in spiritual as in temporal things, remains for ever annulled and utterly extinguished; and We transfer that same jurisdiction and authority wholly and in every way to the ordinaries of the places, according to the cases and persons and under the conditions which We shall explain below; forbidding, as by these presents We do forbid, that anyone henceforth be received into the said Society, or admitted to its habit and novitiate; and those hitherto received may in no way be admitted to the profession of simple or of solemn vows, under pain of the nullity of the admission and profession, and other penalties at our discretion. Moreover We will, enjoin and command that those who are now actually in their novitiate be dismissed at once, forthwith, immediately and effectively; and We likewise forbid that those who have made profession of simple vows and have not yet been initiated into any sacred order may be promoted to those major orders under the pretext or title either of a profession already made in the Society, or of privileges conferred on the same Society contrary to the decrees of the Council of Trent.",
26: "But since our endeavours aim at this, that, as We desire to provide for the interests of the Church and the tranquillity of the peoples, so We may also strive to bring some comfort and help to the individual members of the same religious institute, whose persons We love paternally in the Lord, that, freed from all the contentions, disputes and anxieties by which they have hitherto been harassed, they may cultivate the vineyard of the Lord more fruitfully and profit more richly the salvation of souls: We therefore decree and ordain that the members professed of simple vows only, and not yet initiated into sacred orders, must depart from the houses and colleges of the same Society, released from every bond of their simple vows, within a space of time to be fixed by the ordinaries of the places — sufficient for finding some employment or office, or a benevolent receiver, yet not to begin later than one year from the date of these our present letters — to take up that manner of life which each shall judge in the Lord most suited to his vocation, his strength and his conscience; since indeed by the Society's own privileges they could be dismissed from it for no other cause than such as the superiors judged more conformable to prudence and circumstances, with no previous citation, no acts drawn up, and no judicial order observed.",
27: "To all members promoted to sacred orders We give leave and power either to leave the same houses and colleges of the Society and betake themselves to some one of the regular orders approved by the Apostolic See — where, if they made profession of simple vows in the Society, they will have to complete the time of probation prescribed by the Council of Trent; but if of solemn vows also, they shall stand in probation for only six full months, wherein We benignly dispense with them — or to remain in the world as priests and secular clerics, under the entire and total obedience and subjection of the ordinaries in whose diocese they fix their domicile; decreeing further that to those who shall thus remain in the world there be assigned, until they are otherwise provided for, some suitable stipend out of the revenues of the house or college where they dwelt, regard being had both to the revenues and to the burdens annexed to them.",
28: "But the professed already constituted in sacred orders who — whether led by fear of not having honourable maintenance enough, or through lack of a suitable provision, or because they have no place where to find a domicile, or by reason of advanced age, infirm health, or another just and grave cause — shall judge it inopportune to leave the houses or colleges of the Society, may remain there; yet on this condition, that they have no administration of the said house or college, wear only the dress of secular clerics, and live most fully subject to the ordinary of the place. We moreover absolutely forbid them to put others in the place of those who fail; to acquire any new house or place, according to the decrees of the council of Lyons; or to have power to alienate the houses, effects and places which they now hold. Rather they may be gathered into one house or several, regard being had to the members who remain, so that the houses left empty may be converted to pious uses, according as shall seem, in due place and time, rightly and duly suited to the sacred canons, the will of the founders, the increase of divine worship, the salvation of souls and the public benefit. And meanwhile some man of the secular clergy, endowed with prudence and upright character, shall be designated to preside over the government of the said houses, the name of the Society being wholly deleted and suppressed.",
29: "We declare that the individual members of the said Society from all the provinces from which they are found already expelled are also comprehended in this general suppression of the Society; and therefore We will that the aforesaid expelled members, even if promoted to major orders, unless they pass to another regular order, be by the fact itself reduced to the state of secular clerics and priests, and be totally subject to the ordinaries of the places.",
30: "The ordinaries of the places, if they find in those who by force of these our present letters shall have passed from the regular Institute of the Society of Jesus to the state of secular priests the virtue, learning and integrity of morals that is needed, may at their discretion grant them, or refuse them, the faculty of hearing the sacramental confessions of Christ's faithful and of preaching sacred sermons publicly to the people; without which licence in writing none of them shall dare to exercise those functions. Yet this faculty the same bishops or ordinaries of the places shall never grant, as regards those outside, to those who shall live in colleges or houses formerly belonging to the Society, to whom We accordingly perpetually forbid the administering of the sacrament of penance to outsiders, and preaching, even as Gregory X our predecessor forbade in like manner in the general council cited above. And concerning this We burden the conscience of the bishops themselves, whom We wish mindful of that most severe account which they are to render to God for the sheep committed to their care, and of that hardest judgement also with which the supreme Judge of the living and the dead threatens those who are set over others.",
31: "We will moreover that if any of those who professed the Institute of the Society exercise the office of instructing youth in letters, or act as master in any college or school, they be altogether removed from its direction, administration and government; and that place and power of persevering in the office of teaching be given only to those who give some promise of good hope of their labours, and provided they show themselves strangers to those disputations and heads of doctrine which, by their laxity or their emptiness, are wont to breed and beget the gravest contentions and troubles; and that at no time whatever be admitted to such an office of teaching, or be allowed to continue their work in it if they are now actually engaged in it, those who are not likely to preserve, so far as in them lies, the quiet of the schools and the public tranquillity.",
32: "As to what concerns the sacred missions — with respect to which also We will that whatever We have disposed concerning the suppression of the Society be understood — We reserve to ourselves the establishing of those means by which the conversion of the infidels and the settling of dissensions may be obtained and secured with greater ease and firmness.",
33: "The privileges and statutes whatsoever of the oft-named Society being annulled and utterly abrogated, as above, We declare its members, when they shall have left the houses and colleges of the Society and been reduced to the state of secular clerics, to be capable and fit — according to the decrees of the sacred canons and the apostolic constitutions — to obtain any benefices whatsoever, without cure as with cure, offices, dignities, personates and other things of that kind, to all of which, while they remained in the Society, access had been utterly closed to them by Gregory XIII of happy memory, by his letters in like form of Brief expedited the 10th of September 1584, whose beginning is Satis superque. Likewise We permit them, what had equally been forbidden them, to receive an alms for the celebration of Mass, and to enjoy all those graces and favours of which, as regular clerks of the Society of Jesus, they would have been perpetually deprived. We derogate equally from each and all of the faculties with which, by force of privileges obtained from the Supreme Pontiffs, they had been endowed by the superior general and other superiors — namely of reading the books of heretics and others proscribed and condemned by the Apostolic See; of not observing the days of fasting, or of not using the foods of abstinence on them; of anticipating or postponing the recitation of the canonical hours; and other things of that kind — which We most severely forbid them to use hereafter; since it is our mind and intention that henceforth, as secular priests, they conform their manner of life to the paths of the common law.",
34: "We forbid that, after these our present letters have been promulgated and made known, anyone dare to suspend their execution, even under the colour, title or pretext of any petition, appeal, recourse, declaration or consultation of doubts which might perhaps arise, or any other pretext foreseen or not foreseen. For We will that from this moment and immediately the suppression and annulment of the whole of the said Society and of all its offices take its effect, in the form and manner expressed by Us above, under pain of greater excommunication to be incurred by the fact itself, reserved to Us and to our successors the Roman Pontiffs for the time being, against whoever shall presume to place an impediment, obstacle or delay in the way of the fulfilment of these our letters.",
35: "We command moreover, and in virtue of holy obedience We enjoin, each and every ecclesiastical person, regular and secular, of whatever grade, dignity, quality and condition, and signally those who up to now were enrolled in the Society and counted among its members, not to dare to defend, to attack, to write or even to speak about this suppression, or about its causes and motives, as neither about the Institute of the Society, its rules, constitutions, form of government or any other matter pertaining to this argument, without the express licence of the Roman Pontiff; and in like manner, under pain of excommunication reserved to Us and to our successors for the time being, We forbid each and all, on occasion of this suppression, to dare to wound or provoke anyone — least of all those who were members — with injuries, taunts, insults or any other kind of contempt, by voice or in writing, secretly or openly.",
36: "We exhort all Christian princes that, with that force, authority and power which they have received from God for the defence and patronage of the holy Roman Church, and likewise with that obedience and reverence by which they are drawn toward this Apostolic See, they lend their labour and endeavour that these our letters obtain their fullest effect; and indeed that, adhering to each of the things contained in the same letters, they establish and promulgate similar decrees, by which they may altogether prevent any quarrels, contentions and discords from being stirred up among the faithful while this our will is carried into execution.",
37: "Lastly We exhort all Christians, and beseech them through the bowels of our Lord Jesus Christ, to remember that all have the same Master, who is in heaven; the same Redeemer, by whom we were bought at a great price; that all were regenerated in the same laver of water in the word of life, and made sons of God and co-heirs of Christ; that all were nourished with the same food of Catholic doctrine and of the divine word; that all, finally, are one body in Christ, and severally members one of another; and that therefore it is altogether necessary that all, bound together by the common bond of charity, keep peace with all men, and owe no man anything but to love one another — for he who loves his neighbour has fulfilled the law — pursuing with the utmost hatred offences, rivalries, quarrels, snares and other things of that kind, devised, invented and stirred up by the ancient enemy of the human race to disturb the Church of God and hinder the eternal happiness of the faithful, under the most deceitful title and pretext of schools, of opinions, or even of Christian perfection. Let all, in fine, strive with all their strength to acquire for themselves the true and genuine wisdom of which it is written by Saint James, in the third chapter of his canonical epistle, verse 13: 'Who is a wise man, and endued with knowledge among you? Let him show, by a good conversation, his work in the meekness of wisdom. But if you have bitter zeal, and there be contentions in your hearts, glory not, and be not liars against the truth. For this is not wisdom descending from above, but earthly, sensual, devilish. For where envying and contention is, there is inconstancy and every evil work. But the wisdom that is from above first indeed is chaste, then peaceable, modest, easy to be persuaded, consenting to the good, full of mercy and good fruits, without judging, without dissimulation. And the fruit of justice is sown in peace, to them that make peace.'",
38: "We will also that these present letters can at no time ever be noted, impugned, invalidated or retracted, called into law or controversy, or reduced to the terms of law — not even on the ground that the superiors and other religious of the oft-named Society, and any others whosoever having, or in any way pretending to have, an interest in the premises, did not consent to them, nor were called and heard concerning them — nor on the ground of any vice of surreption, obreption, nullity or invalidity, or of any defect of our intention, or any other defect however great, unthought-of and substantial; nor because the solemnities and whatever else ought to have been observed and fulfilled in the premises, or any of them, were not observed; nor from any other head resulting from law or from any custom, even one contained in the body of the law; nor on the plea of enormous, most enormous and total lesion, or any other pretext, occasion or cause, however just, reasonable and privileged, even such as would necessarily have had to be expressed for the validity of the premises; nor may a restitution in integrum, an opening of the mouth, a reduction to the way and terms of law, or any other remedy of law, of fact, of grace or of justice be sought against them, or, if granted or obtained, may anyone use it or avail himself of it in court or out of it; but that these same present letters exist and shall be always and perpetually valid, firm and efficacious, obtain their plenary and entire effects, and be inviolably observed by each and all whom they concern or shall in any way concern in the future.",
39: "And thus, and not otherwise, in each and all of the premises, do We decree that judgement and decision must be given by any judges whatsoever, ordinary and delegated, even the auditors of causes of the Apostolic Palace and the Cardinals of the Holy Roman Church, even legates a latere and nuncios of the Apostolic See, and any others of whatever authority and power, now and hereafter exercising office, in any cause and instance — every faculty and authority of judging or interpreting otherwise being withdrawn from them and from each of them; and whatever may happen to be attempted to the contrary in these matters, by anyone, with whatever authority, knowingly or in ignorance, We decree null and void.",
40: "Notwithstanding apostolic constitutions and ordinances, even those published in general councils, and, so far as need be, our rule of not taking away a right acquired, and likewise the statutes and customs of the oft-named Society and of its houses, colleges and churches, even strengthened by oath, apostolic confirmation or any other firmness; and the privileges also, the indults and the apostolic letters granted, confirmed and renewed to the same Society, its superiors, religious and any persons whatsoever, under whatever tenors and forms, and with whatever derogatories of derogatories and other decrees even of annulment, even motu proprio, even consistorially, or otherwise in any way. All and each of which — even if for their sufficient derogation a special, express and individual mention of them and of their whole tenors, word for word, and not by general clauses importing the same, or any other exquisite form, ought to be observed for this — holding their tenors as fully and sufficiently expressed and inserted in these presents, as if they were expressed and inserted word for word with nothing at all omitted and the form given in them observed, they otherwise to remain in their force, We specially and expressly derogate for the effect of the premises, and all other things whatsoever to the contrary.",
41: "And We will that to transcripts of these present letters, even printed, subscribed by the hand of some public notary and furnished with the seal of some person constituted in ecclesiastical dignity, entirely the same faith be given, in court and out of it, as would be given to these presents themselves, if they were exhibited or shown. Given at Rome, at Saint Mary Major, under the ring of the Fisherman, the 21st day of July 1773, in the fifth year of our pontificate.",
}
